<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Frederick Barbarossa</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1122">1122</a>–<a href="/years/1190">1190</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hohenstaufen">Hohenstaufen</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Frederick Barbarossa was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-emperor">Holy Roman Emperor</a> in the twelfth century. He sought to assert imperial authority in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, clashing with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papacy">papacy</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombard</a> cities, and he drowned in 1190 while leading an army on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-crusade">Third Crusade</a>. He became a legendary figure in the history of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/frederick-barbarossa.jpg" alt="Frederick Barbarossa" width="256" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Montecappio · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Friedrich_I._Barbarossa.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Prince Levon II, who acceded in 1187, changed that: with the support of the Holy Roman Emperors <b>Frederick Barbarossa</b> and Henry VI he was crowned in the cathedral of Tarsus on 6 January 1198, the Armenian Christmas, by the Catholicos Gregory VI Abirad.</blockquote><a href="/empire/armenian-kingdom-of-cilicia">Armenian Kingdom of Cilicia · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>…a stem duchy of the Empire it passed between families: the Ottonian kings gave it to relatives, the Welfs held it from 1070 with interruptions, and in 1180 <b>Frederick Barbarossa</b> deposed Henry the Lion and granted the duchy to Otto of Wittelsbach, whose family ruled Bavaria until 1918.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-bavaria">Duchy of Bavaria · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>As Polish power fragmented after Bolesław’s death in 1138, the dukes turned towards the German princes: after defeat by Saxon and Danish forces at Verchen in 1164 they submitted to Henry the Lion, and in 1181 Bogislaw I received his duchy as an imperial fief from <b>Frederick Barbarossa</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-pomerania">Duchy of Pomerania · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1176 he refused Emperor <b>Frederick Barbarossa</b> military help in Italy, shortly before the emperor’s defeat at Legnano, and Saxon princes then brought complaints against him.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-saxony">Duchy of Saxony · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1156 Emperor <b>Frederick Barbarossa</b> gave the palatinate to his half-brother Conrad of Hohenstaufen, who built a territorial base on the Rhine and the lower Neckar around Heidelberg and Alzey, together with advocacies over church lands.</blockquote><a href="/empire/electoral-palatinate">Electoral Palatinate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Hamburg celebrates its port's birthday every May on the strength of a charter of <b>Frederick Barbarossa</b> dated 1189 that historians have shown to be a thirteenth-century forgery.</blockquote><a href="/empire/free-and-hanseatic-city-of-hamburg">Free and Hanseatic City of Hamburg · At a glance</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Haguenau, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in River Göksu, in what is now <a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=48.82,7.79&amp;year=1166" aria-label="Haguenau on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4730 459 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Frederick Barbarossa"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5180 729h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5180 729h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=48.82,7.79&amp;year=1166">What was here in 1166? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
